  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 111 AM CDT Thu May 21 2026\n\nThe weather for this weekend through early next week will be driven \nmainly by persistent upper-level troughing over the western US and a \nwarm, moist airmass in the low levels.  This means that the southern \nPlains will be under southwest flow aloft. A more pronounced trough \nover the Desert Southwest and Baja California will eject several \nweak embedded waves eastward into west Texas for late Friday into \nSaturday. At the surface, southerly to southeasterly flow should \ncontinue to advect moist, unstable air into west central Texas. \nChances for storms look lower on Friday after the first upper-\nlevel wave passes. However, plenty of sunshine with warm, moist \nair in place could lead to isolated storm development along the \ndryline in the late afternoon and evening.\n\nThe upper-level trough over the Baja will move eastward into Texas \non Saturday while the surface front drops southward into northwest \nTexas. Thunderstorm chances should increase again for Saturday and \nSunday along and ahead of this front in addition to anything that \nforms on the dryline. Models show this upper-level trough \npotentially becoming a weak cutoff low over east Texas late Sunday \nthrough early next week. This setup would keep west central Texas in \nan active pattern with continuing chances for thunderstorms on \nMonday and Tuesday. The risk of severe weather each day is difficult \nto determine at this time until the picture becomes a bit clearer.",
